WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.687
Path validation decides where an operation
is allowed to point.

2
00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:06.836
Open behavior decides what the program is
allowed to do there.

3
00:00:06.916 --> 00:00:08.392
A secure workflow needs both.

4
00:00:08.472 --> 00:00:12.308
The selected mode or flags should match
the intended operation: read, write,

5
00:00:12.388 --> 00:00:14.572
create, append, replace, or temporary use.

6
00:00:14.652 --> 00:00:18.311
Writable access should not be requested
when read-only access is enough.

7
00:00:18.391 --> 00:00:22.287
A download or preview workflow should not
need write permission to the managed

8
00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:22.839
content.

9
00:00:22.919 --> 00:00:26.696
A validation routine should not open a
file with broad modification rights.

10
00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:30.377
Narrow access reduces accidental effects
and makes code review easier.

11
00:00:30.457 --> 00:00:32.994
Create and replace behavior should be
deliberate.

12
00:00:33.074 --> 00:00:36.498
Creating files in a managed directory,
replacing generated output,

13
00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:39.942
and appending to logs each have different
integrity expectations.

14
00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:43.976
The code should not create or truncate
files as a side effect of a convenient open

15
00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:44.351
mode.

16
00:00:44.431 --> 00:00:48.091
Reviewers should be able to see when the
program may change existing data.

17
00:00:48.171 --> 00:00:51.948
Newly created files should use permissions
appropriate to their contents.

18
00:00:52.028 --> 00:00:54.979
Sensitive reports, private uploads,
temporary extracts,

19
00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:58.896
and internal state should not become
broadly readable because of defaults or

20
00:00:58.976 --> 00:01:00.038
inherited settings.

21
00:01:00.118 --> 00:01:03.777
Permissions are part of the file workflow,
not only a deployment concern.

22
00:01:03.857 --> 00:01:08.107
Least privilege supports traversal
prevention by reducing what a mistake can reach.

23
00:01:08.187 --> 00:01:11.315
The process account, directory
permissions, storage layout,

24
00:01:11.395 --> 00:01:14.523
and application checks should all point in
the same direction.

25
00:01:14.603 --> 00:01:18.794
Path controls are strongest when the
operating system also limits access to only the

26
00:01:18.874 --> 00:01:19.700
approved areas.
